      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "We describe a 77-year-old lady who presented with progressive retrosternal pain radiating to the left arm and the back. After exclusion of cardiac causes a large midoesophageal diverticulum was found on oesophago-gastro-duodenoscopy. Importantly, the retrosternal pain completely disappeared after endoscopic removal of impacted food from the diverticulum. After the surgical resection the patient became fully asymptomatic. This is the first example of angina-like chest pain which definitively resulted from a midoesophageal diverticulum. Therefore, midoesophageal diverticula should be considered as a rare differential diagnosis of exercise-induced retrosternal pain."},
